This paper introduces an optimal control-based approximation of functional-based models that incorporate rigid bodies. Initially, we divide a given domain $O$ into multiple non-overlapping regions $D_1, \ldots, D_k$. Assuming that each $D_i$ remains rigid during the time period $[0,T]$, we can model this using the equation \begin{align*}
\frac{\partial v}{\partial t} - \Delta v &= \mu \quad \text{in } D_i \times (0,T], \\
\nabla v \cdot \mathbf{N} &= 0 \quad \text{on } \Gamma_i \times (0,T],
\end{align*}where $\mu$ represents an given map. Our goal is to find the minimal value for the functional \begin{align*}
\mathcal{J}(v) = \int_{D_i} |v(x)|^2 \, \mathrm{d}x + \beta \int_{D_i} |\nabla v(x)|^2 \, \mathrm{d}x.
\end{align*}To achieve this, we utilize a variational approach based on the finite element method, and demonstrate the existence and uniqueness of the solution for a modified, regularized problem. Furthermore, we prove that the solution converges to the original problem as the regularization parameter increases. This methodology is applicable to any given map $\mu$. Additionally, we extend this approach to account for areas with deformable fluids by introducing a phase-field model. This model involves a thin layer where the fluid's density varies according to the interface. This modification enables the capture of moving boundaries and allows for the approximation of convex functionals defined on piecewise-rigid maps. This work has implications for the study of phase-field models in fluid mechanics and can be further developed to analyze optimal control problems governed by partial differential equations. This research was conducted with the support of the the the Agence Nationale de la Recherche (ANR) through the laboratory of excellence Celya, and the the the Fondation Math\'ematiques de Paris. This research was also conducted while the author was a postdoctoral researcher at the Universit\'e de Paris, under the direction of Professor Emmanuel Fr\'emont. This research was conducted while the author was a postdoctoral researcher at the Universit\'e de Paris, under the direction of Professor Emmanuel Fr\'emont. This research was conducted while the author

